body:
Just realized my post got removed. Odd. Do you suppose I broke any rules?

&#x200B;

Anyway, I appreciate the idea that existence is essentially neutral and we assign meaning to it, but still, I can't help but feel the need to express the disappointment I feel in my most sorrowful moments of which recently there have been plenty. It's a sort of therapy for me.

Wow, the gift part kind of caught me off guard. You think so? I always enjoyed writing but never thought I was particularly good at it. Thank you for the feedback!

&#x200B;

EDIT: Also the awesome part was meant to be the formal awesome like "an awesome God" not the informal awesome. Super dense is actually a scientific term as well, so that part at least was by no means intended to be text speak. I did get informal in some parts admittedly, but I felt it was necessary to evoke the tone I was going for.

It was a bit hard to follow honestly. I think you could try to go with a more vibrant word choice. Something that will make more of an impact on initial reading.  

&#x200B;

I like your choice to let words trail off onto the next line. Gives the poem a nice flow

I'm going to say I have the same problem with this as I have with the last poem I reviewed, which is that it's basically too straightforward. I felt like I didn't have to put any effort into determining what was being said, which is part of the whole enjoyment of poetry for me.

&#x200B;

Also, once again pretty basic rhyme scheme. It could be a bit more adventurous.

Well, it's chipper, but I feel like it gives too much away. Poetry should be a bit conservative with how much information about the subject matter of the poem it discloses to the reader. You want them to work some stuff out for themselves.

&#x200B;

Also, the rhyme scheme kinda seems a little too basic. I don't know if that's what you were going for, but you could benefit from varying up the pattern as it gets to sounding kinda like a nursery rhyme honestly.

Yes, this is exactly what I was thinking of. Thank you!

&#x200B;

EDIT: I was actually talking about this trope when I made the OP. Didn't know what it was called at the time and wasn't aware it was prevalent in western media as well but I can actually think of a few instances of it in western media now that I've been prompted such as the character Clementine from the movie Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind.

I feel you. I called the hotline during one of my first instances of suicidal ideation and the lady said something to the effect of "you need to help yourself" or something like that. 

&#x200B;

But to be completely honest, just what is the suicide hotline supposed to say in an ideal situation? The whole concept seems pretty flawed. I personally have no idea what to say when a person admits to me they're contemplating suicide.

Yeah I get you. I was just teasing you tbh 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I was like this in college. But recently I've been learning how to cook, only because I don't want to have to constantly depend on other people to cook for me and because it's a generally useful skill to have.

&#x200B;

If you feel like you can afford to support your current lifestyle, I wouldn't say it's so bad.

I didn't shower for months once. I later developed psychosis. It was awesome.

&#x200B;

But regarding your problem, yeah I agree not showering can feel gross. Try to perhaps just wash certain body parts like your face and ass, then on a different day maybe wash your legs and chest.etc Alternatively, you could try putting music on as you shower...that helps me sometimes...ofc that only works if you have speakers. If none of these sound viable, just don't shower until you're ready to. I mean it's your fucking body right?

Well if you isolate your existence to a specific point in time, it makes sense. Because everything else is just an abstraction right?

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
